* START-DESCRIPTION:
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account <ADD/DELETE> user.name
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account SSH               - ssh only
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account API               - the API only
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account BOTH              - ssh and the API
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account NONE              - neither
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account SH-ON | SH-OFF    - the SH verb and "!"
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account OS-ON | OS-OFF    - OS.EXECUTE and the screen editors
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account STANDARD | PROGRAMMER | ADMINISTRATOR | SUSPENDED
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account STANDARD | PROGRAMMER  SSH | API | BOTH | NONE
*                                          - when leaving ADMINISTRATOR
*
* THE TIER IS ABSOLUTE TOO, and it moves in any direction with no intermediate
* step: an account can go PROGRAMMER, STANDARD, SUSPENDED and back to any of
* them.  Naming a tier on a suspended account lifts the suspension into that
* tier.  There is no "resume" keyword - owner's ruling, 27 Aug 2026: coming
* back always names the destination.
*
* SUSPENDED DENIES ACCESS AND CHANGES NOTHING ELSE.  The VOC is left exactly as
* it is, no Windows group membership moves, and the tier it displaced is kept
* in ACC$PRIOR.TIER so the VOC delta on the way back knows where the account is
* coming from.  Three doors refuse a suspended account: LOGIN, CPROC's
* logto.authorised and APISRVR's vb.account.
*
* LEAVING ADMINISTRATOR TAKES THREE THINGS AWAY and one of them must be named.
* Windows Administrators membership and the os.users record go automatically -
* the account held both BECAUSE it was an administrator - but ssh and the API
* were a rule and become keyword-set, so the caller says what they are to be
* (10111 if they do not).
*
* THE KEYWORD IS ABSOLUTE.  It says what the account's remote access is, and
* whatever it does not name is withdrawn - so API takes ssh away.  The same
* four words are CREATE.ACCOUNT's, so access is said one way whether an account
* is being made or changed.
*
* AN ADMINISTRATOR IS REFUSED (10083) and a GROUP ACCOUNT is refused (10087).
* Administrators always have both - owner's rule, 21 Aug 2026 - and a group
* account has no Windows user to put in a group; it is reached with LOGTO.
*
* THE FOUR OS FORMS REFUSE AN ADMINISTRATOR TOO (10106), and a group account
* (10105).  Owner's rule, 27 Aug 2026: "administrators have full access, there
* should be no way to turn it off."  They are four switches over two fields
* rather than four names for one state, so SH-OFF leaves OS.EXECUTE alone.
*
* THE TWO REMOTE ROUTES, AND THERE IS NO THIRD.  The console and Remote Desktop
* are denied to every account SD creates - sdsshonly, applied by CREATE.ACCOUNT
* and enforced by gplbld/deny-logon.ps1 - and no verb here lifts that.  An
* administrator reaches the keyboard because Windows already calls them one,
* not because SD granted it.
*
* EACH IS ONE GROUP MEMBERSHIP AND NOTHING IS WRITTEN TO ACCOUNTS.  sdssh is
* what sshd's AllowGroups names; sdapi is what APISRVR tests after the SCRAM
* proof succeeds.  A mirrored field could only ever disagree with the thing
* that actually decides - the reasoning that deleted ACC$USERS on 15 Aug.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program $modify.account
$catalog $MODIFYA

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include parser.h
$include err.h
* 
deffun is_grp_member(user,group) calling '!is_grp_member'
deffun os_group(action,group,member) calling '!os_group'
deffun is_group(group) calling '!is_group'
deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'
deffun ps_script(script) calling '!ps_script'
* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - the tier ordering on grants, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 57.
* The ADD arm below makes the same group change GRANT does, so it takes the
* same refusal; logto.authorised in CPROC is the gate that actually holds.
deffun tier_allows(user,account) calling '!tier_allows'

   prompt ''

   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end     

   parser = "!PARSER"

* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - ASSIGNED HERE FOR BCOMP, NOT FOR THE LOGIC.  LOGIN
*   carries the same block for the same reason and says it plainly: "is not
*   assigned a value" is a compile WARNING, and bootstrap.py line 229 fails the
*   WHOLE bootstrap on one.  BCOMP reads the source in order, so a variable set
*   in a routine that stands textually BELOW the routine reading it counts as
*   unassigned however the control flow actually runs.
*
*   THE TWO THAT ACTUALLY NEED IT are rank.out, which voc.delta reads and
*   tier.rank sets forty lines further down, and the route.apply trio, which
*   tier.set reads.  The rest are here because listing only the two that fail
*   today invites the next routine to be inserted in the wrong place and fail
*   the bootstrap with no clue why.
*
*   EVERY VALUE IS ALSO THE SAFE ONE.  rank.out is 2 - PROGRAMMER, which is
*   what a blank tier means (SYSCOM/KEYS.H) - and the counters are zero, so a
*   path that somehow skipped the work reports having done nothing rather than
*   reporting somebody else's numbers.
   want.tier          = ''
   rank.in            = ''
   rank.out           = 2
   tier.dir           = ''
   tier.id            = ''
   tier.got           = @false
   tier.rec           = ''
   tier.layer.rec     = ''
   tier.from.template = @false
   tmpl.open          = @false
   route.failed       = @false
   route.same         = @false
   route.stat         = 0
   voc.ok             = @true
   voc.added          = 0
   voc.removed        = 0
   voc.kept           = 0
   voc.skipped        = @false
*  29 Aug 26 Windows port - the promotion report, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 57.  Here
*  for the reason the banner above gives: promo.report reads what
*  promo.snapshot sets, and both stand textually BELOW tier.set, which is what
*  BCOMP counts as unassigned.  promo.readable is @false so a path that never
*  reached the snapshot reports "not checked" rather than a confident zero.
   promo.was          = ''
   promo.lost         = ''
   promo.n            = 0
   promo.checked      = 0
   promo.readable     = @false
   promo.mem          = ''
   promo.rc           = 0
   promo.count        = 0
   promo.i            = 0
   promo.who          = ''

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS     ;* Preset for argument errors
   
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f else
      @system.return.code = -status()
      stop sysmsg(6000) ;* Cannot open ACCOUNTS file in SDSYS account
   end

   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword) ;* Verb modify.Account

   * Get next token, account name
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, acc.name, keyword)
   if token.type = PARSER$END then
      crt 'Command Syntax:  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> ADD | DELETE <username>'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SSH | API | BOTH | NONE'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SH-ON | SH-OFF'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> OS-ON | OS-OFF'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> STANDARD | PROGRAMMER'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> ADMINISTRATOR | SUSPENDED'
      stop
   end
   *
   acc.name = upcase(acc.name)   ;* account names are always upper case
   if acc.name = 'SDSYS' then
      stop sysmsg(2202) ;* Account name is invalid
   end   
   *
   read acc.rec from acc.f, acc.name else      
      stop sysmsg(6003) ;* Account name is invalid
   end
   
   group.name = acc.rec<ACC$GROUP>   ;* get associated group
   
   if is_group(group.name) then
      * Get next token, ADD or DELETE
      call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, action, keyword)

* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - SSH, API, BOTH and NONE.  Owner's decision, 21 Aug
*   2026, replacing the SSH / NO.SSH / API / NO.API pairs of earlier the same
*   day.  Four actions in the slot ADD and DELETE occupy:
*
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SSH    ssh only
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> API    the API only
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> BOTH   both
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> NONE   neither
*
*   THE KEYWORD SAYS WHAT THE ACCESS IS, NOT WHAT TO CHANGE, and that is the
*   whole difference from the pairs it replaces.  "API" used to mean "add the
*   API to whatever it already had"; it now means "the API and nothing else",
*   so it WITHDRAWS ssh.  The same four words are CREATE.ACCOUNT's, so an
*   account's access is said the same way whether it is being made or changed.
*
*   WHICH IS WHY THE MESSAGE STATES THE WHOLE RESULTING STATE rather than what
*   moved.  Someone who types API meaning BOTH has just taken ssh away, and a
*   message reading "may now use the API" would not tell them - it is true and
*   it is the wrong half.  10076-10079 each name both routes.
*
*   They take NO user name - they act on the account's OWN Windows user - so
*   they are handled before the ADD/DELETE test rather than inside it, which
*   would demand one.
*
*   THE PROPERTY IS GROUP MEMBERSHIP AND NOTHING ELSE.  Nothing is written to
*   ACCOUNTS - owner's decision, 20 Aug 2026, kept - so there is no record that
*   could disagree with the group.
*
*   WRAPPED ROUND THE ADD/DELETE BLOCK RATHER THAN FOLDED INTO IT.  That block
*   is working code with four message paths in it; turning its "if ADD or
*   DELETE" into arms of a case statement would rewrite all of it to add
*   actions that share none of it.  One "if not(route.done)" leaves it
*   untouched.
      route.done = @true
      want.ssh = @false
      want.api = @false
      begin case
         case upcase(action) = 'SSH'
            want.ssh = @true
            gosub route.set
         case upcase(action) = 'API'
            want.api = @true
            gosub route.set
         case upcase(action) = 'BOTH'
            want.ssh = @true
            want.api = @true
            gosub route.set
         case upcase(action) = 'NONE'
            gosub route.set

* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - OPERATING-SYSTEM ACCESS, BOTH WAYS ROUND.  Owner,
*   27 Aug 2026: "sh-on and sh-off", "os-on and os-off".  CREATE.ACCOUNT takes
*   the two ON forms; the OFF forms are only meaningful once an account exists,
*   which is what this verb is for.
*
*   FOUR KEYWORDS OVER TWO FIELDS, NOT AN ABSOLUTE STATE.  SSH/API/BOTH/NONE
*   above name what the access IS and each one sets both groups; these are two
*   independent switches, so "sh-off" says nothing about OS.EXECUTE and leaves
*   it alone.  That is why os.set takes a field number rather than a pair of
*   wants.
         case upcase(action) = 'SH-ON'
            os.field = 1
            os.want  = @true
            gosub os.set
         case upcase(action) = 'SH-OFF'
            os.field = 1
            os.want  = @false
            gosub os.set
         case upcase(action) = 'OS-ON'
            os.field = 2
            os.want  = @true
            gosub os.set
         case upcase(action) = 'OS-OFF'
            os.field = 2
            os.want  = @false
            gosub os.set

* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - THE TIER, WHICH WAS A CREATE-TIME PROPERTY UNTIL
*   TODAY.  Owner, 27 Aug 2026: "an administrator account should be able to be
*   downgraded to programmer or standard", and a fourth level, SUSPENDED, "so
*   that an account can be temporarily denied access".
*
*   FOUR NAMES FOR ONE STATE, like SSH/API/BOTH/NONE and unlike the OS
*   switches.  The keyword says what the account IS, and the same three words
*   are CREATE.ACCOUNT's - SUSPENDED is the one only this verb can set, because
*   there is no reason to create an account already denied.
*
*   IN ANY DIRECTION, AND WITH NO INTERMEDIATE STEP.  His question, and it
*   decided the shape: an account can go programmer -> standard -> suspended
*   and back to any of them, never needing to be un-suspended first.  Naming a
*   tier on a suspended account lifts the suspension into that tier.
         case upcase(action) = 'STANDARD'
            want.tier = 'STANDARD'
            gosub tier.set
         case upcase(action) = 'PROGRAMMER'
            want.tier = 'PROGRAMMER'
            gosub tier.set
         case upcase(action) = 'ADMINISTRATOR'
            want.tier = 'ADMINISTRATOR'
            gosub tier.set
         case upcase(action) = 'SUSPENDED'
            want.tier = 'SUSPENDED'
            gosub tier.set

         case 1
            route.done = @false
      end case

      if not(route.done) then
      if (upcase(action) = 'ADD') or (upcase(action) = 'DELETE') then
      *  valid action
      *   Get next token, user name
         call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, user.name, keyword) 
         if token.type = PARSER$END then
            stop sysmsg(10016)  ;* Missing User Name
         end
         
         if (upcase(action) = 'ADD') then

* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - THE TIER ORDERING APPLIES HERE TOO, AND LEAVING IT
*   OUT WOULD HAVE MADE THIS VERB THE WAY ROUND IT.  Owner's rule, 29 Aug
*   2026, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 57: a grant may go down or sideways, never up.
*   This arm makes the identical os_group('ADDMEM', ...) call GRANT makes -
*   which is exactly why PRE_RELEASE 27 had to give it the same audit record -
*   so it needs the same refusal for the same reason.  Two verbs that change
*   who may enter an account must not disagree about who may.
*
*   BEFORE THE sdusers AND "already a member" TESTS.  A membership that should
*   not exist must not be reported as a cheerful "already", which is the one
*   answer that stops anybody looking at it.
*
*   DELETE IS DELIBERATELY NOT GUARDED: taking access away is always allowed,
*   and refusing a revoke would strand exactly the memberships this rule wants
*   removed.
            if not(tier_allows(user.name, acc.name)) then
               ma.why = status()
               @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
               begin case
                  case ma.why = 4
                     stop sysmsg(10126, acc.name, 'SDSYS', user.name)
                  case ma.why = 2
                     stop sysmsg(10127, user.name)
                  case 1
                     stop sysmsg(10126, acc.name, ~
                                 upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$TIER>, ' ', 'B')), ~
                                 user.name)
               end case
            end

            * Add user to group
            * does user belong to sdusers?
            if is_grp_member(user.name,'sdusers') then
            * this user looks legit, add to group
            * first check to see if user is already a member of the group
               if is_grp_member(user.name,group.name) THEN
                  crt sysmsg(10017,user.name,group.name)  ;* %1 is already a member of group %2
               end else
                  * Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
                  * Use shared !valid_os_name before os.execute (phase 2).
                  * if convert(os.name.chars, '', user.name) # '' ...
                  if not(valid_os_name(user.name)) or not(valid_os_name(group.name)) then
                     crt sysmsg(10019,user.name,group.name,2) ;* Unable to add user %1 to group %s status: %2
                  end else
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - was: os.execute "sudo usermod -aG ..."
                  stat = os_group("ADDMEM", group.name, user.name)
                  * --------------------
                  if stat = 0 then
* 28 Aug 26 Windows port - AUDIT IT, the way GRANTA:208 does.  This arm makes
* the identical os_group('ADDMEM', ...) call that GRANT makes and wrote
* nothing, so who may enter an account could be changed by two verbs and only
* one of them left a trail.  AFTER the edit and inside "stat = 0", so it
* records what happened rather than what was attempted.  The verb is named in
* the text because the two routes are otherwise indistinguishable in the audit
* file.  The identity is stamped by audit_message() in k_error.c and is not
* passed from here, so a caller cannot forge it.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 27.
                     void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'MODIFY.ACCOUNT ADD account=' : acc.name : ' to=' : user.name)
                     crt sysmsg(10018,user.name,group.name)  ;* %1 added to group %2
                  end else
                     crt sysmsg(10019,user.name,group.name,stat) ;* Unable to add user %1 to group %s status: %2
                  end
                  end
               end 
            end else
               crt user.name
               crt sysmsg(10020,user.name) ;* %1 is not a member of sdusers, not allowed
            end 
         
         end else
            * already tested action, must be delete
            if is_grp_member(user.name,group.name) THEN
               * Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
               * Use shared !valid_os_name before os.execute (phase 2).
               if not(valid_os_name(user.name)) or not(valid_os_name(group.name)) then
                  crt sysmsg(10022,user.name,group.name,2) ;* Unable to remove user %1 from group %s status: %2
               end else
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - was: os.execute "sudo deluser ..."
               stat = os_group("DELMEM", group.name, user.name)
               * --------------------
                  if stat = 0 then
* 28 Aug 26 Windows port - and the DELETE arm, for the reason given at the ADD
* one above.  GRANTA:233 is the model.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 27.
                     void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'MODIFY.ACCOUNT DELETE account=' : acc.name : ' from=' : user.name)
                     crt sysmsg(10021,user.name,group.name) ;*  %1 removed from group %2
                  end else
                     crt sysmsg(10022,user.name,group.name,stat) ;* Unable to remove user %1 from group %s status: %2
                  end
               end
            end else
               crt sysmsg(10023,user.name,group.name) ;* %1 is not a member of  %2
            end 
          
         end
         
         
      end else
         crt 'MODIFY.ACCOUNT Action Must Be ADD, DELETE, SSH, API, BOTH, NONE,'
         crt '   SH-ON, SH-OFF, OS-ON, OS-OFF, STANDARD, PROGRAMMER,'
         crt '   ADMINISTRATOR or SUSPENDED'
      end
      end       ;* not(route.done)

   end else
     crt sysmsg(10024,group.name) ;* %1 is not a valid Windows group
   end
   goto program.end

* ======================================================================
* The account's OWN Windows user.
*
* 20 Aug 26 Windows port - DERIVED FROM ACC$GROUP, NOT FROM THE RECORD ID.
*   CREATE.ACCOUNT downcases the user name (CREATEA) and writes the ACCOUNTS
*   record under the UPPER case name, so downcase(acc.name) would usually be
*   right - but "usually" is doing real work there and this decides whether
*   somebody can log in.  ACC$GROUP is "sdu_":acc.uname, written by
*   CREATE.ACCOUNT from the user name itself, so taking the name back out of it
*   is a reversal of what was actually recorded rather than a guess at it.
*
*   AND THE RESULT IS CHECKED AGAINST sdusers before anything acts on it, with
*   the same test the ADD path above already uses.  That is what rejects a
*   GROUP-type account: its ACC$GROUP is a name the caller supplied, not
*   "sdu_"-anything, so the name that comes out is not an SD user and the
*   check refuses it.  A GROUP account has no single Windows user for this to
*   mean anything about.
account.user:
   acc.user = ''
   if group.name[1,4] = 'sdu_' then acc.user = group.name[5,999]
   if acc.user = '' then
      crt sysmsg(10020,acc.name) ;* %1 is not a member of sdusers, not allowed
      user.ok = @false
      return
   end
   user.ok = is_grp_member(acc.user,'sdusers')
   if not(user.ok) then
      crt sysmsg(10020,acc.user) ;* %1 is not a member of sdusers, not allowed
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* SSH / API / BOTH / NONE - set this account's remote access to exactly what
* was asked for, by making its own Windows user's membership of sdssh and
* sdapi match want.ssh and want.api.
*
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - ONE ROUTINE, AND IT IS ABSOLUTE.  It replaced
*   route.add and route.remove, which each moved ONE group in ONE direction and
*   between them needed eight message numbers.  The keyword now says what the
*   access IS, so there is one operation - make the world match - and it is
*   idempotent by construction rather than by asking first.
*
*   IT REPORTS THE RESULTING STATE, NOT THE TRANSITION, for the reason given at
*   the case statement above: with an absolute keyword the interesting fact is
*   what the account can do now, and half of that may be something the caller
*   did not realise they were withdrawing.
*
*   "NOTHING CHANGED" IS STILL SAID SEPARATELY (10080).  Reporting the state
*   alone would leave a caller unable to tell a no-op from a change, and this
*   is a verb people run twice when they are not sure it took.
*
*   ADMINISTRATORS ARE REFUSED, and this guard is NEW.  Owner's rule, 21 Aug
*   2026: "all administrators have access to both ssh and api ... the whole
*   purpose of ADMINISTRATOR is that it gives unlimited access."  So there is
*   nothing here for them to set, and silently ignoring the request would be
*   worse than refusing it - the caller would believe the access had narrowed.
*   BY SID, like CREATEA's test, because BUILTIN\Administrators is translated
*   on a localised Windows.
*
*   NO sdsshonly GUARD, for the reason the routines this replaced gave: that
*   group carries SeDenyInteractiveLogonRight and no verb in this program
*   touches it.  sdssh cannot lock an administrator out of ssh either, because
*   allow-ssh-groups.ps1 names the Administrators group in AllowGroups in its
*   own right.  If a future route ever writes to sdsshonly, the guard returns.
*
*   THE WITHDRAWAL IS IMMEDIATE AND THE GRANT MAY NOT BE.  sshd re-reads its
*   AllowGroups per connection and APISRVR asks the SAM per login, so neither
*   consults a token; but a Windows access token fixes group membership at
*   sign-in, so an account GRANTED a route mid-session may still need to sign
*   out and back in before anything token-based agrees.
route.set:
   * A GROUP ACCOUNT HAS NO REMOTE ROUTE TO SET.  It has no Windows user at all
   * - CREATE.ACCOUNT's GROUP arm makes an sdg_ group and reaches neither
   * create_user() nor set_passwd() - and it is reached with LOGTO or an F
   * pointer.  Said plainly here, because account.user below would otherwise
   * answer "is not a member of sdusers", which is true and explains nothing.
   if group.name[1,4] = 'sdg_' then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10087, acc.name) ;* %1 is a group account and has no remote access
      return
   end

   gosub account.user
   if not(user.ok) then return

   if is_grp_member(acc.user, "S-1-5-32-544") then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10083, acc.user) ;* %1 is an administrator and always has both
      return
   end

   gosub route.apply

   if route.failed then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10081, acc.user, route.stat) ;* Unable to change remote access
      return
   end

   if route.same then
      crt sysmsg(10080, acc.user) ;* %1 already had that access; nothing changed
      return
   end

   gosub say.access
   return

* ======================================================================
* route.apply  -  make acc.user's sdssh and sdapi membership match want.ssh and
* want.api.  Sets route.failed, route.same and route.stat; prints nothing.
*
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - SPLIT OUT OF route.set, unchanged, because tier.set
* needs the same three group moves and must NOT inherit route.set's guards.
* An administrator is refused by route.set (10083) and a tier change is the one
* operation where an account's administrator-ness is itself what is moving, so
* the guard has to sit above this and not inside it.
*
* IT PRINTS NOTHING, which is the reason it is a separate routine rather than a
* flag.  route.set reports the resulting state; tier.set reports the tier and
* would otherwise print two verdicts about one command.
route.apply:
   route.failed = @false
   route.same   = @false
   route.stat   = 0

   if not(valid_os_name(acc.user)) then
      route.failed = @true
      route.stat = 2
      return
   end

   has.ssh = is_grp_member(acc.user, 'sdssh')
   has.api = is_grp_member(acc.user, 'sdapi')

   if (has.ssh = want.ssh) and (has.api = want.api) then
      route.same = @true
      return
   end

   if has.ssh # want.ssh then
      if want.ssh then
         route.stat = os_group("ADDMEM", 'sdssh', acc.user)
      end else
         route.stat = os_group("DELMEM", 'sdssh', acc.user)
      end
   end

   * The API half runs only if the ssh half succeeded.  A failure part way
   * leaves the account in a state neither the caller nor this message
   * describes, so it is reported with the status and NOT summarised as though
   * it had worked.
   if route.stat = 0 then
      if has.api # want.api then
         if want.api then
            route.stat = os_group("ADDMEM", 'sdapi', acc.user)
         end else
            route.stat = os_group("DELMEM", 'sdapi', acc.user)
         end
      end
   end

   if route.stat # 0 then route.failed = @true
   return

* ======================================================================
* SH-ON / SH-OFF / OS-ON / OS-OFF - set ONE field of this account's own
* Windows user's OS.USERS record to yes or no.
*
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - owner, 27 Aug 2026.  os.field is 1 for SH - the SH
* verb and "!" at the prompt, read by CPROC - and 2 for OS.EX, read by
* op_sh.c's os_permitted() for OS.EXECUTE and by EDIT's check.permitted for the
* two screen editors.
*
* ***AN ADMINISTRATOR IS REFUSED HERE, THE SAME WAY route.set REFUSES ONE.***
* Owner, 27 Aug 2026: "administrators have full access, there should be no way
* to turn it off."  All three routes are a RULE for an administrator - ssh, the
* API and the operating system - and none of them is this verb's to change.
*
* THAT REVERSES WHAT THIS BANNER SAID FOR ONE DAY, and the argument it made is
* recorded because it was a reasonable one that he overruled: operating-system
* access looked like a DEFAULT, since CREATE.ACCOUNT grants it unasked, against
* ssh and the API which are granted by rule.  He ruled it is a rule.
*
* BOTH DIRECTIONS, not just the OFF forms, which is also what route.set does.
* "os-on" for an administrator is asking this verb to set something it does not
* decide, and answering "done" to it would be a lie about where the state comes
* from.
*
* THE COST, SAID RATHER THAN HIDDEN: an administrator adopted over a Windows
* user who already had an os.users record saying "no" keeps that record -
* grant.os.access leaves an existing one exactly as it is (10103) - and no verb
* can now change it.  The record is ordinary data and an elevated session
* passes every gate that reads it, so "ed os.users <name>" from SDSYS is the
* way out.  Page 26 of the SD TCL reference already names it.
*
* A MISSING RECORD READS AS "no" IN BOTH FIELDS, exactly as both gates read it,
* so turning something off that was never on is "nothing changed" and writes
* nothing.  The only way to get a record that says no twice is to have had one
* say yes, which is a fact worth keeping rather than tidying away.
*
* READU AND RELEASE, because two administrators editing OS.USERS at once is
* rare and silent corruption of a permission list is not a thing to leave to
* chance.  The no-op path releases without writing.
os.set:
   if group.name[1,4] = 'sdg_' then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10105, acc.name) ;* %1 is a group account and has no Windows user
      return
   end

   gosub account.user
   if not(user.ok) then return

*  AN ADMINISTRATOR IS REFUSED, exactly as route.set refuses one.  Owner,
*  27 Aug 2026: "administrators have full access, there should be no way to
*  turn it off."  BY SID, so the two guards cannot drift and neither depends on
*  a group name a localised Windows translates.
*
*  ITS OWN MESSAGE, NOT 10083.  That one says "always has both ssh and the
*  API", which is true and is about the other two routes; a caller told it
*  after typing "os-off" would read it as the wrong refusal.
   if is_grp_member(acc.user, "S-1-5-32-544") then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10106, acc.user)
      * %1 is an administrator and always reaches the operating system
      return
   end

   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'os.users' to osu.f else
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10104, acc.user) ;* Unable to change operating-system access
      return
   end

   readu osu.rec from osu.f, acc.user else osu.rec = ''

   os.was = (upcase(trim(osu.rec<os.field>, ' ', 'B')) = 'YES')
   if os.was = os.want then
      release osu.f, acc.user
      close osu.f
      crt sysmsg(10080, acc.user) ;* %1 already had that access; nothing changed
      return
   end

   os.new = 'no'
   if os.want then os.new = 'yes'
   osu.rec<os.field> = os.new

*  BOTH FIELDS ARE REWRITTEN, in the two spellings this program uses, so a
*  record hand-edited to "YES" or "Yes" or "y" comes out of here saying what
*  the gates will actually read.  The gates fold case and compare to "yes"; a
*  record that says "y" has always meant no and would otherwise keep looking
*  as though it meant something.
   osu.sh = 'no'
   osu.ex = 'no'
   if upcase(trim(osu.rec<1>, ' ', 'B')) = 'YES' then osu.sh = 'yes'
   if upcase(trim(osu.rec<2>, ' ', 'B')) = 'YES' then osu.ex = 'yes'
   osu.rec = osu.sh
   osu.rec<2> = osu.ex

   osu.ok = @true
   write osu.rec to osu.f, acc.user on error osu.ok = @false
   close osu.f

* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - AND THE SAME FALLBACK CRED_SET HAS.  PRE_RELEASE 68.
*
*   os.users is READ-ONLY to sdusers - secure-osusers.ps1 grants (OI)(CI)(RX) -
*   so this write is refused by Windows for a session that reached SDSYS with
*   LOGTO from an unelevated start, however administrator SD believes it is
*   after CPROC:2769.  Same cause as the $cred write, same route out: ps_script
*   hands the work to the elevated helper when K$ADMINISTRATOR is set
*   (PS_SCRIPT:166) and falls back to os.execute when it is not, so an ordinary
*   caller behaves exactly as before.  The long version of the reasoning is in
*   CRED_SET, including why CRLF and why ASCII are both measured facts.
*
*   AFTER close, DELIBERATELY.  The readu above holds an update lock and close
*   releases it; letting an outside process write the file while SD holds a lock
*   on the record would be a lock SD believes in and Windows knows nothing about.
   if not(osu.ok) then
      osu.path = kernel(K$WINPATH, @sdsys : @ds : 'os.users' : @ds : acc.user)
      if osu.path # '' then
         q = char(39)
         ps =       '$p = ' : q : osu.path : q
         ps := @fm : '$l = @(' : q : osu.sh : q : ',' : q : osu.ex : q : ')'
         ps := @fm : '$nl = [char]13 + [char]10'
         ps := @fm : 'try {'
         ps := @fm : '  [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($p, ($l -join $nl) + $nl,'
         ps := @fm : '     [System.Text.Encoding]::ASCII)'
         ps := @fm : '} catch { exit 1 }'
         ps := @fm : 'exit 0'

         if ps_script(ps) = 0 then osu.ok = @true
      end
   end

*  READ BACK BEFORE REPORTING, for CRED_SET's reason: the elevated path runs a
*  script whose exit code says the FILE was written and nothing about whether SD
*  can read it back, and this file is the whole of the OS.EXECUTE gate.
   if osu.ok then
      osu.ok = @false
      openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'os.users' to osu.chk then
         read osu.back from osu.chk, acc.user then
            if upcase(trim(osu.back<1>, ' ', 'B')) = upcase(osu.sh) then
               if upcase(trim(osu.back<2>, ' ', 'B')) = upcase(osu.ex) then
                  osu.ok = @true
               end
            end
         end
         close osu.chk
      end
   end

   if not(osu.ok) then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10104, acc.user)
      return
   end

   crt sysmsg(10102, acc.user, osu.sh, osu.ex) ;* os.users: %1 has SH %2, OS.EXECUTE %3
   return

* ======================================================================
* STANDARD / PROGRAMMER / ADMINISTRATOR / SUSPENDED - change the account's tier.
*
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - owner, 27 Aug 2026.  The tier was a create-time
* property until today; ACC$TIER outlived CREATE.ACCOUNT but nothing could
* change it.  His two rulings: an administrator account may be downgraded, and
* there is a fourth level, SUSPENDED, that denies access temporarily.
*
* THE ORDER OF WORK IS CHOSEN FOR WHAT A FAILURE LEAVES BEHIND.  Windows first,
* then the VOC, and the register LAST.  Every step before the register write is
* idempotent - a group the user is already in, a record already written, a VOC
* entry already copied or already gone - so a run that dies part way leaves the
* register saying the OLD tier and re-running the same command converges.  The
* register first would leave the opposite: a record claiming a tier the account
* does not have, which is the failure ACC$TIER exists to prevent.
*
* FIELD 6 IS WRITTEN ONLY ON THE WAY IN TO SUSPENDED, and it is the EQUALITY
* GUARD at the top of tier.set that makes that true - a second
* "modify.account x suspended" returns there (sysmsg 10110) and never reaches
* the field-6 write, so it cannot overwrite the real prior tier with SUSPENDED.
* Any move to a named tier clears it, because it has then been used.  There was
* an inner "if old.tier # 'SUSPENDED'" at the write as well; it was unreachable
* and is gone (PRE_RELEASE 21).  See SYSCOM/KEYS.H.
*
* NOTHING IS WITHDRAWN ON WINDOWS FOR A SUSPENSION, which is the owner's
* ruling and is what makes lifting one free.  The stated cost is in LOGIN
* beside the refusal: the ssh connection is still accepted and SD refuses the
* login, rather than sshd refusing the connection.
tier.set:
   old.tier   = upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$TIER>, ' ', 'B'))
   prior.tier = upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$PRIOR.TIER>, ' ', 'B'))
   grp.acct   = (group.name[1,4] = 'sdg_')

*  THE EQUALITY GUARD.  It also protects field 6: a second suspend of an
*  already-suspended account returns here, so the field-6 write below never
*  sees old.tier = 'SUSPENDED'.  PRE_RELEASE 21.
   if old.tier = want.tier then
      crt sysmsg(10110, acc.name, want.tier) ;* %1 is already %2; nothing changed
      return
   end

*  A GROUP ACCOUNT CAN HOLD ANY TIER BUT ADMINISTRATOR.  Its VOC is built the
*  same way and it can be suspended - LOGTO is how it is reached and CPROC
*  refuses a suspended one - but ADMINISTRATOR means a Windows group
*  membership and there is no single person behind a group account to put in
*  it.  CREATE.ACCOUNT's GROUP arm reaches neither create_user() nor
*  set_passwd() for the same reason.
   if grp.acct and want.tier = 'ADMINISTRATOR' then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10105, acc.name) ;* %1 is a group account and has no Windows user
      return
   end

*  DO NOT LET SOMEBODY SUSPEND THEIR WAY OUT OF THE BUILDING.  Both names are
*  tested because they can differ: @logname is the account you log in to and
*  the one this would lock, @who is the account you are standing in after a
*  LOGTO.  Suspending either from inside it leaves a session that works until
*  it ends and then cannot be re-established, and the only way back is
*  "sd -internal", which is undocumented and elevated.
   if want.tier = 'SUSPENDED' then
      if (acc.name = upcase(@logname)) or (acc.name = upcase(@who)) then
         @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
         crt sysmsg(10112) ;* You cannot suspend your own account or the one you are standing in
         return
      end
   end

   acc.user  = ''
   was.admin = @false
   if not(grp.acct) then
      gosub account.user
      if not(user.ok) then return
*     BY SID AND NOT BY THE TIER FIELD, the same test route.set uses.  It is
*     also the only test that is right here: an account suspended while it was
*     an administrator still carries the Windows membership, so its ACC$TIER
*     reads SUSPENDED while Windows still calls it an administrator.  Asking
*     Windows answers "what is actually true" in both cases.
      was.admin = is_grp_member(acc.user, "S-1-5-32-544")
   end

*  A DOWNGRADE OUT OF ADMINISTRATOR MUST NAME THE ACCESS, and this is the
*  owner's ruling of 27 Aug 2026 rather than a default chosen here.  An
*  administrator has ssh and the API as a rule; below that tier they are
*  keyword-set, so the downgrade has to land somewhere and nothing here is
*  entitled to pick.  SUSPENDED is exempt: it withdraws nothing on Windows, so
*  there is no state for the caller to choose.
   need.access = @false
   if was.admin then
      if want.tier # 'ADMINISTRATOR' and want.tier # 'SUSPENDED' then
         need.access = @true
      end
   end

   if need.access then
      call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, access.word, keyword)
      want.ssh  = @false
      want.api  = @false
      access.ok = @true

      if token.type = PARSER$END then
         access.ok = @false
      end else
         begin case
            case upcase(access.word) = 'SSH'
               want.ssh = @true
            case upcase(access.word) = 'API'
               want.api = @true
            case upcase(access.word) = 'BOTH'
               want.ssh = @true
               want.api = @true
            case upcase(access.word) = 'NONE'
               want.ssh = @false
               want.api = @false
            case 1
               access.ok = @false
         end case
      end

      if not(access.ok) then
         @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
         crt sysmsg(10111, acc.name)
         * Say what remote access %1 is to have: ssh, api, both or none
         return
      end
   end

*  ---- Windows first.  See the banner: everything before the register write
*  ---- is idempotent, so a failure here leaves the old tier standing.
   tier.stat = 0

   if want.tier = 'ADMINISTRATOR' and not(grp.acct) and not(was.admin) then
      tier.stat = os_group("ADDMEM", "S-1-5-32-544", acc.user)
   end

   if tier.stat = 0 and need.access then
      tier.stat = os_group("DELMEM", "S-1-5-32-544", acc.user)
   end

   if tier.stat # 0 then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10114, acc.name) ;* Unable to change the tier of %1
      return
   end

*  ---- The two routes and the operating system, for the two tier moves that
*  ---- change them.  A suspension changes neither.
   if want.tier = 'ADMINISTRATOR' and not(grp.acct) then
      want.ssh = @true
      want.api = @true
      gosub route.apply
      if route.failed then
         @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
         crt sysmsg(10081, acc.user, route.stat)
         return
      end
      if not(route.same) then gosub say.access
      gosub tier.os.write
   end

   if need.access then
      gosub route.apply
      if route.failed then
         @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
         crt sysmsg(10081, acc.user, route.stat)
         return
      end
      if route.same then
         crt sysmsg(10080, acc.user)
      end else
         gosub say.access
      end
      gosub tier.os.remove
   end

*  ---- The VOC.  Owner's ruling: at once, not at the next login.
   voc.from = old.tier
   if voc.from = 'SUSPENDED' then voc.from = prior.tier
   voc.to = want.tier

*  A SUSPENSION LEAVES THE VOC EXACTLY AS IT IS.  A suspended account cannot be
*  entered, so its VOC decides nothing while the suspension lasts, and leaving
*  it alone is what makes the round trip lossless: the delta on the way back is
*  computed from field 6 against the tier the caller names.
   if voc.to = 'SUSPENDED' then voc.to = voc.from

   voc.ok      = @true
   voc.added   = 0
   voc.removed = 0
   voc.kept    = 0
   voc.skipped = @false

   if old.tier = 'SUSPENDED' and prior.tier = '' then
*     Field 6 empty on a suspended account means the record was hand-edited or
*     written before this change.  There is no honest "from" tier, and guessing
*     one would either strip verbs the account is entitled to or add verbs it
*     is not.  Say so and leave the VOC alone.
      voc.skipped = @true
   end else
      gosub voc.delta
   end

   if not(voc.ok) then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10114, acc.name)
      return
   end

*  ---- WHO CAN ENTER THIS ACCOUNT TODAY?  The BEFORE half of the promotion
*  ---- report, taken while the register still says the OLD tier.  It must be
*  ---- the last thing before the write and it changes nothing.
   gosub promo.snapshot

*  ---- The register, last.
   readu tier.rec.new from acc.f, acc.name else tier.rec.new = acc.rec

   tier.rec.new<ACC$TIER> = want.tier
   if want.tier = 'SUSPENDED' then
*     old.tier is necessarily NOT 'SUSPENDED' here: the equality guard at the
*     top of tier.set returned already if it were.  That guard, not an inner
*     test, is what stops a second suspend overwriting field 6.  PRE_RELEASE 21.
      tier.rec.new<ACC$PRIOR.TIER> = old.tier
   end else
      tier.rec.new<ACC$PRIOR.TIER> = ''
   end

   tier.written = @true
   write tier.rec.new to acc.f, acc.name on error tier.written = @false

   if not(tier.written) then
      release acc.f, acc.name
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10114, acc.name)
      return
   end

   crt sysmsg(10109, acc.name, want.tier) ;* Account %1 is now %2

   if voc.skipped then
      crt sysmsg(10108, acc.name)
      * %1 has no record of the tier it held, so its VOC was left alone
   end else
      crt sysmsg(10113, voc.added, voc.removed, voc.kept)
      * VOC: %1 records added, %2 removed, %3 left alone
   end

*  ---- The AFTER half.  Last, because it is a consequence of the change and
*  ---- the change should be reported before its consequences.
   gosub promo.report

   return

* ======================================================================
* promo.snapshot / promo.report  -  name the grants this tier change voided.
*
* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 57's last piece.  Put to the owner
* as two options - refuse the promotion while lower-tier members remain, or let
* it proceed and say what it broke - and answered in five words on 29 Aug 2026:
* "Proceed, and print what it voided."
*
* WHAT IT IS FOR.  "modify.account b programmer" raises the account's rank, and
* every standard-tier member of sdu_b is then somebody LOGTO will refuse.  The
* gate holds - that is what TIERGATE is for - but the membership sits in
* Windows looking perfectly valid and simply stops working, with nothing said
* at the moment it stopped.
*
* ***IT IS MEASURED ACROSS THE WRITE, NOT PREDICTED FROM THE RANKS.***
* tier_allows reads ACC$TIER out of the register, so the SAME call answers
* differently either side of the register write above.  That makes "what did
* this command void" a genuine before-and-after comparison instead of rank
* arithmetic duplicated out of TIERGATE - which is the copy that would go
* stale, and TIERGATE's own note says why one place has to decide.
*
* SO IT CLAIMS ONLY WHAT THIS COMMAND CHANGED.  A membership that was ALREADY
* refused before the command ran - made with "net localgroup", or left behind
* by an earlier promotion - is not this command's doing and is not listed as
* though it were.  GRANTA's note on the same subject is why such memberships
* exist at all.
*
* A DEMOTION VOIDS NOTHING AND PRINTS NOTHING, and that falls out rather than
* being special-cased: lowering the account's rank cannot turn an allowed
* member into a refused one, so the comparison is empty.  Nothing here tests
* the direction of the move, which is one fewer thing to get wrong.
*
* ***AND THE NULL CASE IS REFUSED OUT LOUD.***  "No grant was voided" and "the
* group could not be read" are the same silence otherwise, and the second is
* the one somebody needs to hear, because it means the quiet is not evidence.
* promo.readable is tested before promo.n for exactly that reason.

promo.snapshot:
   promo.was      = ''
   promo.lost     = ''
   promo.n        = 0
   promo.checked  = 0
   promo.readable = @false

*  An account with no group has no grants to void.  Not an error, and not a
*  "could not read" either - there is nothing to read.  Reported as readable
*  with an empty list, which is the truth.
   if group.name = '' then
      promo.readable = @true
      return
   end

   promo.mem = ''
   promo.rc  = os_group('LISTMEM', group.name, promo.mem)
   if promo.rc # 0 then return

   promo.readable = @true

*  dcount('') IS 1, NOT 0.  GRANTA's do.list carries the same two lines for
*  the same reason; without the correction an empty group reports one member
*  whose name is the empty string.
   promo.count = dcount(promo.mem, @fm)
   if promo.mem = '' then promo.count = 0

   for promo.i = 1 to promo.count
      promo.who = trim(promo.mem<promo.i>, ' ', 'B')
      if promo.who # '' then
         if tier_allows(promo.who, acc.name) then
            promo.was<-1> = promo.who
         end
      end
   next promo.i

   return

promo.report:
   if not(promo.readable) then
      crt sysmsg(10129, group.name, acc.name)
      * The members of %1 could not be read, so grants on %2 are unchecked
      return
   end

   promo.checked = dcount(promo.was, @fm)
   if promo.was = '' then promo.checked = 0

   for promo.i = 1 to promo.checked
      promo.who = promo.was<promo.i>
      if not(tier_allows(promo.who, acc.name)) then
         promo.lost<-1> = promo.who
      end
   next promo.i

   promo.n = dcount(promo.lost, @fm)
   if promo.lost = '' then promo.n = 0

   if promo.n = 0 then return

   crt sysmsg(10128, promo.n, acc.name, want.tier)
   for promo.i = 1 to promo.n
      crt '   ' : promo.lost<promo.i>
   next promo.i

   return

* ======================================================================
* voc.delta  -  bring the account's VOC from tier voc.from to tier voc.to.
*
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - A DELTA AND NOT A REBUILD, and that is forced rather
* than chosen.  CREATE.ACCOUNT builds a VOC into an empty directory and then
* writes the account's file pointers into it; re-running that build on a live
* account would copy NEWVOC over the top of every F pointer the account has
* accumulated.  What can be done safely is to move only the records the two
* tiers disagree about.
*
* THE THREE TIERS NEST, which is what makes a delta expressible at all:
*   STANDARD       NEWVOC less TIER.OMIT.STANDARD
*   PROGRAMMER     NEWVOC entire
*   ADMINISTRATOR  NEWVOC entire, plus TIER.ADD.ADMINISTRATOR from VOC_TEMPLATE
* so STANDARD is a subset of PROGRAMMER is a subset of ADMINISTRATOR, and the
* difference between any two is one or both of the two named lists.  Going up
* copies a layer in; going down takes it out.
*
* THE LISTS ARE READ, NOT REPRODUCED.  TIER.OMIT.STANDARD and
* TIER.ADD.ADMINISTRATOR live in NEWVOC and are the same records CREATE.ACCOUNT
* and LOGIN read.  This routine holds the tier ARITHMETIC and no tier data, so
* a verb added to either list reaches all three readers at once.
*
* ***A DOWNGRADE DELETES ONLY WHAT IT WOULD HAVE WRITTEN.*** Each id is rebuilt
* from the source file exactly as CREATE.ACCOUNT would have built it and
* compared with what the account's VOC actually holds; a record that differs is
* one somebody has edited or replaced, and it is COUNTED AND LEFT.  Deleting by
* id alone would silently destroy a user's own work under a verb's name, and
* the count is reported so "left alone" is visible rather than assumed.
voc.delta:
   if voc.from = voc.to then return

   rank.in = voc.from
   gosub tier.rank
   from.rank = rank.out

   rank.in = voc.to
   gosub tier.rank
   to.rank = rank.out

   if from.rank = to.rank then return

   tier.path = parse.pathname.tokens(acc.rec<ACC$PATH>)

   openpath tier.path:@ds:'voc' to tvoc.f else
      voc.ok = @false
      return
   end

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'newvoc' to tnewvoc.f else
      close tvoc.f
      voc.ok = @false
      return
   end

   tmpl.open = @false

   if to.rank > from.rank then
      tier.dir = 'ADD'

      if from.rank < 2 then
         read tier.layer.rec from tnewvoc.f, 'TIER.OMIT.STANDARD' else tier.layer.rec = ''
         tier.from.template = @false
         gosub tier.layer
      end

      if to.rank = 3 then
         read tier.layer.rec from tnewvoc.f, 'TIER.ADD.ADMINISTRATOR' else tier.layer.rec = ''
         gosub tier.open.template
         if tmpl.open then
            tier.from.template = @true
            gosub tier.layer
         end
      end
   end else
      tier.dir = 'DEL'

      if from.rank = 3 then
         read tier.layer.rec from tnewvoc.f, 'TIER.ADD.ADMINISTRATOR' else tier.layer.rec = ''
         gosub tier.open.template
         if tmpl.open then
            tier.from.template = @true
            gosub tier.layer
         end
      end

      if to.rank < 2 then
         read tier.layer.rec from tnewvoc.f, 'TIER.OMIT.STANDARD' else tier.layer.rec = ''
         tier.from.template = @false
         gosub tier.layer
      end
   end

*  THE CLOSE IS A GOSUB AND NOT A "close tvoctmpl.f", for the BCOMP reason at
*  the top of this program: tvoctmpl.f is opened in tier.open.template, which
*  stands below here, so naming the file variable at this point would be its
*  first textual appearance and would read as unassigned.  A gosub to a label
*  below is ordinary and costs nothing.
   if tmpl.open then gosub tier.close.template
   close tnewvoc.f
   close tvoc.f
   return

* ======================================================================
* tier.rank  -  1 STANDARD, 2 PROGRAMMER, 3 ADMINISTRATOR.
*
* BLANK IS 2 AND THAT IS DELIBERATE, matching SYSCOM/KEYS.H and LOGIN's
* update.voc: a blank ACC$TIER means the pre-tier full VOC, which is a
* programmer's set.  Reading it as STANDARD would make the first tier change an
* account ever saw delete 42 verbs it has always had.
tier.rank:
   rank.out = 2
   begin case
      case rank.in = 'STANDARD'      ; rank.out = 1
      case rank.in = 'ADMINISTRATOR' ; rank.out = 3
   end case
   return

* ======================================================================
* tier.open.template  -  open SDSYS VOC_TEMPLATE, once, for the administrator
* layer.  Its failure is not fatal to the whole delta: the standard layer may
* already have been applied, and reporting a partial move truthfully beats
* undoing work that was correct.
tier.open.template:
   if tmpl.open then return
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'voc_template' to tvoctmpl.f then
      tmpl.open = @true
   end else
      voc.ok = @false
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* tier.close.template  -  the other half, and it is a routine only so that
* every mention of tvoctmpl.f stands below the openpath that assigns it.  See
* the note at voc.delta's call to it.
tier.close.template:
   close tvoctmpl.f
   tmpl.open = @false
   return

* ======================================================================
* tier.layer  -  walk one list record and add or delete each id it names.
* Field 1 of both list records is a description, so the walk starts at 2 - the
* same bound CREATE.ACCOUNT and LOGIN use.
tier.layer:
   layer.count = dcount(tier.layer.rec, @fm)
   if layer.count > 1 then
      for layer.i = 2 to layer.count
         tier.id = tier.layer.rec<layer.i>
         if tier.dir = 'ADD' then
            gosub tier.add.one
         end else
            gosub tier.del.one
         end
      next layer.i
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* tier.build.rec  -  read tier.id from the layer's source file and apply the
* type-letter transformation CREATE.ACCOUNT applies on the way into a VOC.
*
* THE TRANSFORMATION IS COPIED FROM CREATEA VERBATIM and must stay that way:
* it is what makes a rebuilt record byte-comparable with one CREATE.ACCOUNT
* wrote, which is the whole basis of the "delete only what we would have
* written" rule in voc.delta.
*
* THE SOURCE FILE IS CHOSEN BY A FLAG RATHER THAN PASSED, because assigning one
* file variable to another is not a thing this codebase does anywhere and this
* is not the place to find out whether it works.
tier.build.rec:
   tier.got = @false

   if tier.from.template then
      read tier.rec from tvoctmpl.f, tier.id then tier.got = @true
   end else
      read tier.rec from tnewvoc.f, tier.id then tier.got = @true
   end

   if tier.got then
      tier.rec<1> = if upcase(tier.rec[1,1]) = 'P' then tier.rec<1>[1,2] else tier.rec[1,1]

      if upcase(tier.rec<1>) = 'K' and tier.rec<3> # '' then
         tier.s = tier.rec<3>
         tier.rec<3> = if upcase(tier.s[1,1]) = 'P' then tier.s<1>[1,2] else tier.s[1,1]
      end
   end

   return

* ======================================================================
* tier.add.one  -  copy one id into the account's VOC.  An id the source file
* does not hold is counted as nothing rather than reported: the lists and the
* files they name are shipped together, and a mismatch is a packaging fault
* that verify-tiers already measures.
tier.add.one:
   gosub tier.build.rec
   if tier.got then
      write tier.rec to tvoc.f, tier.id
      voc.added = voc.added + 1
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* tier.del.one  -  remove one id from the account's VOC, but only if what is
* there is what a tier build would have put there.  See voc.delta's banner.
tier.del.one:
   gosub tier.build.rec
   if tier.got then
      read tier.old.rec from tvoc.f, tier.id then
         if tier.old.rec = tier.rec then
            delete tvoc.f, tier.id on error null
            voc.removed = voc.removed + 1
         end else
            voc.kept = voc.kept + 1
         end
      end
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* tier.os.write  -  an account being made an ADMINISTRATOR reaches the
* operating system, both fields, whatever the record said before.
*
* IT OVERWRITES AN EXISTING RECORD, which is the opposite of CREATE.ACCOUNT's
* grant.os.access, and the difference is that the tier is MOVING here.  There,
* an existing "no" is somebody's decision about an account that already exists
* and a create-time default has no business overruling it.  Here somebody has
* just said "make this an administrator", and by the owner's rule of 27 Aug
* 2026 an administrator always reaches the operating system.
tier.os.write:
   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'os.users' to tosu.f else
      crt sysmsg(10104, acc.user)
      return
   end

   tosu.rec    = 'yes'
   tosu.rec<2> = 'yes'

   tosu.ok = @true
   write tosu.rec to tosu.f, acc.user on error tosu.ok = @false
   close tosu.f

   if tosu.ok then
      crt sysmsg(10102, acc.user, 'yes', 'yes')
      * os.users: %1 has SH %2, OS.EXECUTE %3
   end else
      crt sysmsg(10104, acc.user)
   end

   return

* ======================================================================
* tier.os.remove  -  a downgraded administrator loses the record the tier gave
* them.  Owner's ruling, 27 Aug 2026: it exists BECAUSE they are an
* administrator, so it goes when that does.  DELETE.ACCOUNT removes it the same
* way, with the same "on error null" - an absent record is the wanted state.
tier.os.remove:
   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'os.users' to tosu.f else
      crt sysmsg(10104, acc.user)
      return
   end

   delete tosu.f, acc.user on error null
   close tosu.f

   crt sysmsg(10115, acc.user) ;* os.users: the record for %1 is removed
   return

* ======================================================================
* Say what the account can reach now.  Shared with nothing today, but the same
* four messages are CREATE.ACCOUNT's, which is the point of them.
say.access:
   begin case
      case want.ssh and want.api
         crt sysmsg(10078, acc.user) ;* may sign in over ssh and use the API
      case want.ssh
         crt sysmsg(10076, acc.user) ;* ssh, and may not use the API
      case want.api
         crt sysmsg(10077, acc.user) ;* the API, and may not sign in over ssh
      case 1
         crt sysmsg(10079, acc.user) ;* no remote access at all
   end case
   return

program.end:
   END
